# Greek and Hebrew via babel. These are not in the recording above: on a minimal
# installation babel falls back to its own el/he locale data and never opens
# them. They are listed because they must not be left to chance -- any fuller
# TeX Live has babel-greek, and then greek.ldf is loaded instead of the locale.
#
# greek-fontenc is the one that matters. babel-greek declares no dependency on
# it, so a TeX Live carrying babel-greek without it fails on the Greek sample --
# and fails opaquely, because babel cannot even format its own diagnostic:
#
#     ! Missing \endcsname inserted.
#     \bbl@error #1->\csname bbl@err@#1\endcsname
#
# The message it was trying to print is "Font support for the Greek script
# missing. Install the greek-fontenc package". Verified locally: with all three
# present the PDF builds with no errors and embeds exactly the same faces --
# Gentium, Ezra SIL, Noto Sans CJK TC, TeX Gyre Schola -- as the locale path.
babel-greek
babel-hebrew
greek-fontenc
